1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,388
Some things logic does badly: storing kilobits, and multiplying wide numbers.

2
00:00:05,708 --> 00:00:11,156
Real FPGAs add hard blocks for them, and so does bob, modelled on AMD's own.

3
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:14,651
bob has 2 block RAMs.

4
00:00:14,971 --> 00:00:24,331
Each is a subset of AMD's RAMB18 from UG473: 1024 words of 18 bits, as one physical array.

5
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:35,484
It has two complete ports, A and B, each with its own address, data in, write enable, enable, reset and output-register enable, and its own data out.

6
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:41,762
Both ports can read or write the same array in the same cycle: that is what true dual port means.

7
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:47,504
When a port writes, what should its output show on that same clock edge?

8
00:00:47,824 --> 00:00:53,445
UG473 offers three answers, and bob implements all three, per port.

9
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:55,865
Write first shows the new data.

10
00:00:56,185 --> 00:00:58,870
Read first shows what was there before the write.

11
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:02,293
And no change leaves the output exactly as it was.

12
00:01:02,613 --> 00:01:07,411
In every mode the memory holds the new word afterwards; only the output differs.

13
00:01:07,867 --> 00:01:18,620
bob builds the other two modes from read first, in the way Vivado infers a real RAMB18, so the host chip gives it a genuine block RAM rather than eighteen kilobits of LUTs.

14
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:32,692
So the configuration of a block RAM is just 8 bits: a write mode for each port, an output register for each port, and two bits that let JTAG drive a port directly, which the board tests use.

15
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:36,602
One distinction matters a great deal.

16
00:01:36,922 --> 00:01:40,160
The eight configuration bits say how the RAM behaves.

17
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:47,019
Its contents, eighteen thousand bits, are data, and AMD keeps them apart from configuration.

18
00:01:47,339 --> 00:01:48,384
So does bob.

19
00:01:48,867 --> 00:02:02,359
Contents arrive one of two ways: through a dedicated JTAG register called USER4, or, since milestone fifteen, as frames of their own inside the configuration stream, protected by the same checksum.

20
00:02:02,679 --> 00:02:05,816
Keeping two independent paths caught several bugs.

21
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:08,638
And contents survive a JPROGRAM.

22
00:02:08,958 --> 00:02:15,417
An early loader wrote only up to the last non-zero word, so a new design could read the previous design's data.

23
00:02:15,737 --> 00:02:17,353
Now every word is written.

24
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:26,307
Each of the 2 DSP slices is a trimmed copy of AMD's DSP48E1 from UG479.

25
00:02:26,627 --> 00:02:30,688
Four operand buses come in: A, B, C and D.

26
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:33,933
A pre-adder can form D plus or minus A.

27
00:02:34,253 --> 00:02:38,836
The result is multiplied by B in a twenty-five by eighteen signed multiplier.

28
00:02:39,156 --> 00:02:47,156
The product, M, goes into a forty-eight-bit adder whose other input is chosen by the operation mode, and lands in the P register.

29
00:02:47,633 --> 00:02:52,106
Every stage has an optional pipeline register, as on the real slice.

30
00:02:52,426 --> 00:03:00,754
And P cascades into the next slice's PCIN over a dedicated wire, so two slices can build a filter without touching the routing.

31
00:03:02,333 --> 00:03:19,899
The real DSP48 chooses its operation every cycle from control pins. bob's slice chooses it once, in configuration, from four modes: multiply, multiply-add, multiply-accumulate, and the shifted cascade that joins two slices into one wider multiplier.

32
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:28,461
Dynamic control would need more pins, which means more routing and more configuration, for features a fabric this size will not use.

33
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:34,224
This is one of the places where bob deliberately simplifies its reference, and says so.

34
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:47,872
The whole slice is configured by 16 bits: the operation mode, the pre-adder's use and sign, six optional registers, and the bits that let a JTAG instruction drive its inputs for testing.

35
00:03:48,192 --> 00:03:52,545
Synthesis maps every multiplication in a design onto it automatically.

36
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:01,579
Both cores are written in Vivado's inference style, so they become a real block RAM and a real DSP slice in the host.

37
00:04:01,899 --> 00:04:08,586
Their testbenches walk every mode against the Python model, and the board checks repeat the same vectors over JTAG.

